---
.title = "How to build a full IDE in Neovim from scratch (0.12)",
.date = .date("2026-08-30T00:00:00"),
.layout = "post.shtml",
---

Welcome! In this tutorial we will go over how to create a modern IDE inside of Neovim from scratch. You will learn how to structure your configuration, install and configure plugins, and set up core parts of an IDE such as fuzzy file finding and code completion. I've created a [tutorial repository](https://github.com/trevorhauter/neovim-2026-tutorial) you can use as reference while you follow along.

This tutorial assumes you are familiar with the basics of vim motions. If you are unfamiliar or get stuck, keep this handy [Neovim cheatsheet](https://neovimcheatsheet.com/) close by for reference. Now let's jump right in!

First things first, let's [install Neovim](https://neovim.io/doc/install/). Once you've done that, you can launch neovim inside a terminal with the `nvim` command.

# Creating project structure

Next, let's create a directory to store our configuration. 

```sh
mkdir ~/.config/nvim
```

This is where neovim will look every time it's launched. I recommend storing this in version control, either with your other dotfiles or in a repository of its own. To prevent overloading of the term "configuration", I'm going to refer to the files inside the `~/.config/nvim` directory as our *project* going forward.

Let's establish our core project structure. This will keep things nice and organized as we expand/modify our project!

We're going to create a directory for our core configuration options (native nvim settings), another for plugins, and an init file to tell Neovim where to look when everything is loaded up. We can create all of these by running the following commands inside our root project directory `~/.config/nvim`

```sh
touch init.lua && mkdir lua \
&& mkdir lua/config && touch lua/config/options.lua \
&& mkdir lua/plugins && touch lua/plugins/init.lua
```

Which yields the following tree structure

```
~/.config/nvim
├── init.lua
└── lua
    ├── config
    │   └── options.lua
    └── plugins
        └── init.lua
```

Now, when neovim launches, it will look in `init.lua` at our project root. Let's point `init.lua` to `lua/config/options.lua`, which is going to be the central file that loads everything else we care about.

```lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua
require("config.options")
```

Now, let's add some of our first settings into our `lua/config/options.lua` file!

```lua
-- ~/.config/nvim//lua/config/options.lua
vim.g.mapleader = " " -- Prefix used for keybinds

require("plugins") -- Will load plugins directory automatically

-- Set default indentation length (in spaces)
vim.bo.shiftwidth = 2
vim.bo.softtabstop = 2
vim.bo.tabstop = 2

-- Use spaces for tab in insert mode ;)
vim.opt.expandtab = true

-- Don't highlight every search result when performing a `/` search
vim.opt.hlsearch = false

-- Show line numbers in editor
vim.opt.nu = true

-- Make line numbers relative to cursor (really great for efficient motions!)
vim.opt.relativenumber = true

-- Always keep 8 lines of buffer at the top or bottom
vim.opt.scrolloff = 8

-- Leaves space for signs and other hints in line number column
vim.opt.signcolumn = "yes"

-- Better indentation
vim.opt.smartindent = true

-- I have swapfiles disabled for convenience, 
-- may want to enable if memory is a concern
vim.opt.swapfile = false

-- Wrap text at edge of screen
vim.opt.wrap = true
```

This will get us started with some good defaults. If you aren't sure what a setting does and would like to know, simply type `:h $setting` inside Neovim to open up the documentation, or use a search engine to learn more. There's no right settings to have here, this is just where I've landed personally, and is a fine starting point.

Next, in `lua/plugins/init.lua`, let's add some simple code so that when we create new files in the plugins directory, Neovim loads them in automatically. This will make managing our project a lot easier! :-)

```lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/init.lua
-- Require all files in directory
local config_path = vim.fn.stdpath("config")
local directory_name = "plugins"

for _, file in
    ipairs(
        vim.fn.readdir(
            config_path .. "/lua/" .. directory_name,
            [[v:val =~ '\.lua$']]
        )
    )
do
    local file = file:gsub("%.lua$", "")

    if file ~= "init" then
        require(directory_name .. "." .. file)
    end
end
```

Nice! At this point, we have our structure in place, and are ready to add our first plugin!

# Installing plugins

The plugin ecosystem in Neovim is rich and diverse, and is one of the things that makes the editor so great. You can find tools and solutions for just about any workflow, allowing you to have an editor that is truly yours. Some even refer to Neovim as a PDE (personal development environment) for this very reason.

For our first plugin, let's install a theme. My favorite is [gruvbox-material](https://github.com/sainnhe/gruvbox-material), which is actually the theme used in the syntax highlighting in this post, but you can choose whatever you like!

Let's create a file in our plugins directory to configure our theme

First, let's create our file

```sh
# From ~/.config/nvim
touch lua/plugins/theme.lua
```

Now add our configuration to it

```lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/theme.lua
vim.pack.add({ "https://github.com/sainnhe/gruvbox-material" })

if vim.fn.has("termguicolors") == 1 then
    vim.opt.termguicolors = true
end

-- Theme specific plugin options
vim.g.gruvbox_material_enable_italic = true
vim.g.gruvbox_material_enable_bold = true

-- Can be used in tandem with a transparent terminal 
-- setup to make Neovim transparent!
vim.g.gruvbox_material_transparent_background = 1
vim.o.background = "dark"

vim.g.gruvbox_material_background = "soft"
vim.g.gruvbox_material_better_performance = 1

-- Important! This tells neovim what colorscheme to use
vim.cmd.colorscheme("gruvbox-material")
```

The next time you open Neovim, you should be prompted to install your theme. Confirm the download, relaunch and wow! You've just installed your very first plugin, and your snazzy new theme looks amazing! This will be the general flow for installing and using new plugins.

But wait, what are these options I've set? How did I know I needed them? Well, to answer this, we should look at our themes [repository](https://github.com/sainnhe/gruvbox-material). For all plugins, instructions on what options are available, what the defaults are, and how to change them are provided (or linked to) in the README. I encourage you to read through the documentation for plugins you are interested in, as they will have everything you need to get up and running quickly! As for what to change when you see each plugin, your preferences will vary, there's no right answer! The joy of experimenting and finding what is right for you is what makes Neovim such a great editor.

Now, let's focus on project navigation. Every great IDE needs a file explorer, and we need look no further than [nvim-tree.lua](https://github.com/nvim-tree/nvim-tree.lua)

Let's create a file for our plugin and add our configuration.

```sh
# From ~/.config/nvim
touch lua/plugins/nvim-tree.lua
```

```lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/nvim-tree.lua
vim.pack.add({
    -- web-devicons are a dependency for nvim-tree, but also many other plugins
    { src = "https://github.com/nvim-tree/nvim-web-devicons" },
    { src = "https://github.com/nvim-tree/nvim-tree.lua" },
})

-- Turn off netrw (builtin Neovim file explorer)
vim.g.loaded_netrw = 1
vim.g.loaded_netrwPlugin = 1

require("nvim-tree").setup({
    view = {
        -- Adapt size of explorer to file/directory names
        adaptive_size = true,
        -- Show relative line numbers in file explorer
        number = true,
        relativenumber = true,
    },
    -- Hide git/gitignore by default
    filters = { custom = { "^.git$", "^.gitignore$" } },
})


local nvim_tree_api = require("nvim-tree.api")

-- Toggle nvim-tree in normal mode with ctrl + n
vim.keymap.set(
    "n",
    "<C-n>",
    nvim_tree_api.tree.toggle,
    { noremap = true, silent = true }
)

-- Open your current file in nvim-tree with <leader> + n
vim.keymap.set(
    "n",
    "<leader>n",
    function()
        nvim_tree_api.tree.find_file({
            open = true,
            update_root = "<bang>",
            focus = true,
        })
    end,
    { noremap = true, silent = true }
)
```

After installing our plugins, we now have a beautiful file explorer right inside of neovim! Let's go over a few details of what we've just created. For each of these keymaps, the first argument is the value, "n". This means that each keybind will work only in normal mode. The second argument is the keybind that triggers this new feature, and the third is the function it triggers. We can always find our current file in nvim-tree with `<leader>n`, (remember, we mapped "space" to "leader"), which is really great for jumping to neighboring files, or orienting ourselves in a codebase!

Now let's kick it up a notch. Let's add the powerful search plugin [fzf.lua](https://github.com/ibhagwan/fzf-lua). This will allow us to easily search for files, use grep (or [ripgrep](https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep)) to search for patterns and phrases, or easily filter/search results from commands like `git status` to easily find the files you were just working in!

Before we set up our plugin configuration, we'll need to install a few dependencies. ([Learn more about fzf.lua dependencies](https://github.com/ibhagwan/fzf-lua#dependencies))

- [fzf (required)](https://github.com/junegunn/fzf#installation)
- [ripgrep (optional)](https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep#installation) - better `grep` utility
- [fd (optional)](https://github.com/sharkdp/fd#installation) - better `find` utility

Now that we have those installed, let's add the configuration for our plugin.

```sh
# From ~/.config/nvim
touch lua/plugins/fzf.lua
```

```lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/fzf.lua

vim.pack.add({ "https://github.com/ibhagwan/fzf-lua" })

local fzf = require("fzf-lua")

-- Live grep project
vim.keymap.set({ "n", "v" }, "<leader>/", fzf.live_grep)

-- Search files returned from git status
vim.keymap.set({ "n", "v" }, "<leader>d", fzf.git_status)

-- Search files throughout the project
vim.keymap.set({ "n", "v" }, "<leader>f", fzf.files)

-- Search for the highlighted phrase, or the word under your cursor
vim.keymap.set({ "n", "v" }, "sw", fzf.grep_cword)
```
Wow, that was easy! Now we can easily find what we need in our project with a few simple keybinds. Be sure to check out the [fzf.lua commands](https://github.com/ibhagwan/fzf-lua#commands) to see many more commands you can add to make your editor even more powerful.

You may notice our keymaps look slightly different. Here, the first argument is an array with two values "n" & "v". This means the keybinds will work in normal /and/ visual mode. Type `:h vim.keymap.set` to learn more about the different ways you can set keybinds!

Lastly, let's install a very important plugin [nvim-treesitter](https://github.com/nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter). This will give us better syntax highlighting and enable lots of little quality of life features like snippets, context queues, and more in our editor. [Read more about Treesitter on Learn nvim](https://vonheikemen.github.io/learn-nvim/feature/treesitter.html#what-s-treesitter)

First off, we must install our [dependencies](https://github.com/nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter#requirements). Once we've done that, we're ready to add our configuration!

```sh
# From ~/.config/nvim
touch lua/plugins/nvim-treesitter.lua
```

```lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/nvim-treesitter.lua
vim.pack.add({ "https://github.com/nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter" })

local treesitter = require("nvim-treesitter")

treesitter.install({"python", "zig"})

-- auto-highlight files when opened
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("FileType", {
    callback = function(args)
        if
            vim.list_contains(
                treesitter.get_installed(),
                vim.treesitter.language.get_lang(args.match)
            )
        then
            vim.treesitter.start(args.buf)
        end
    end,
})
```

This will automatically install the Treesitter parsers for the languages you choose. View the [nvim-treesitter documentation](https://github.com/nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter/blob/main/SUPPORTED_LANGUAGES.md) to see a list of available parsers.

There are a number of other quality of life plugins I like to add to my editor. To keep this post from being too long, I will leave a list of recommended plugins and their configurations in our tutorial repository below. Feel free to pick and choose whatever seems interesting to you!

- [eyeliner.nvim](https://github.com/jinh0/eyeliner.nvim) | [(configuration)](https://github.com/trevorhauter/neovim-2026-tutorial/commit/92403e8471df94300376457b72b0505c01a717ed) - Visual plugin that shows f/F indicators
    - See note below for explanation on color overrides

**Note**: Some of these configurations, such as the one for eyeliner.nvim include (optional) color overrides, because the default colors do not provide enough contrast with the recommended theme for this project. This is achieved with a separate `lua/config/colors.lua` file, which is loaded in after everything at the bottom of `lua/options.lua`. This ensures that the color overrides are applied after all the plugins have been loaded in. You may also see entries in a file called `nvim-pack-lock.json`. This is automatically generated and managed by Neovim, and you can safely ignore them.

# Installing and configuring language servers

I hope you're sitting down, because now we get to the really fun part. Adding code completion and intellisense to our editor! 
